<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Felix zu Schwarzenberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1800–1852</span> · Also known as Felix of Schwarzenberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Felix zu Schwarzenberg was an Austrian prince and statesman who became minister-president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> in November 1848, during the revolutions of that year. He installed the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a> as emperor, crushed the Hungarian revolution with Russian help, restored centralised rule and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> to back down at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olmütz</a> in 1850. He died in office in 1852.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/felix-zu-schwarzenberg.jpg" alt="Felix zu Schwarzenberg" width="320" height="549" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Franz Schrotzberg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schwarzenberg,_Felix.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
